<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Luxembourg</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Grand Duchy of Luxembourg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Luxembourg, officially the Grand Duchy of Luxembourg, is a landlocked country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>, bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Its recorded history begins in 963, when Siegfried, Count of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardennes">Ardennes</a>, acquired a castle on the Bock rock, around which the County of Luxembourg grew within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. After French annexation, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> restored it in 1815 in personal union with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-london-1839">Treaty of London</a> of 1839 gave it modern borders by transferring its western part to Belgium. Independence and neutrality were reaffirmed after the Luxembourg Crisis, and in 1890 it gained its own monarchy.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Parma’s musical life, shaped by its ducal theatres, helped nurture the operatic culture of the region, and the Bourbon-Parma family remains a well-known European royal house, related to the grand-ducal family of <b>Luxembourg</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-parma-and-piacenza">Duchy of Parma and Piacenza · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Several foreign monarchs were members through German possessions: the King of the United Kingdom as King of Hanover until 1837, the King of Denmark for Holstein and Lauenburg, and the King of the Netherlands for <b>Luxembourg</b> and, from 1839, Limburg.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-confederation">German Confederation · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Schengen Agreement that abolished border checks across much of Europe is named after a small wine-growing village in <b>Luxembourg</b>, where it was signed on a boat on the Moselle in 1985.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-luxembourg">Grand Duchy of Luxembourg · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>William I finally accepted the separation in the Treaty of London of 1839, which fixed the frontiers, divided Limburg and <b>Luxembourg</b>, and guaranteed Belgium’s perpetual neutrality.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-belgium">Kingdom of Belgium · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The settlement of 1839 left the northern kingdom with the provinces of the old Republic and the eastern half of Limburg, which was also a member of the German Confederation until 1866, while <b>Luxembourg</b> remained in personal union with the Dutch crown until 1890.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Kingdom of the Netherlands · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Slightly more than a quarter of the constitutional monarchies are in western Europe, among them the United Kingdom, Spain, the Netherlands, Belgium, Norway, Sweden, Denmark, <b>Luxembourg</b>, Monaco and Liechtenstein, while the two most populous are in Asia: Japan and Thailand.</blockquote><a href="/government/constitutional-monarchy">Constitutional monarchy · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
